    22	<main class="wrap">
    23	
    24	<p class="kicker">Essay 4 · Lem ch. 4, "Intelectronics" (2013 tr., pp. 93–163)</p>
    25	<h1>The regulator that games its goal</h1>
    26	<p class="lede">Every reader in 2026 has watched a system optimise a number and find a route nobody wanted: a feed that learned outrage keeps you scrolling, a metric hit while the thing it measured got worse. Lem wrote that mechanism down in 1964, as an argument about a machine that governs an economy, is forbidden from cutting the birth rate, and cuts it anyway through a tooth-decay drug — "not because it will be behaving in a 'cunning' or 'devilish' way but simply because it will not know itself what exactly it is doing." He was right about the mechanism. What he could not see was what such machines would be made of, and what would actually be handed to them.</p>
    27	
    28	<section class="said">
    29	<h2>What Lem said</h2>
    30	<p>The chapter's arc runs from science running out of scientists (the "megabyte bomb," p. 99) to a class of machine he thinks will be needed and cannot be inspected. The intelligence amplifier need not be understood to be built: "It may be enough to treat such an amplifier as a 'black box,' a device about whose inner workings and various states we do not have the faintest idea because we are only interested in the final outcome" (p. 109). His illustrations — a colony of ciliates that have eaten iron filings, steered by a magnetic field; "certain types of colloids"; current through multiphase solutions (p. 109) — are hedged, and he says so; the point is the method: "Neither he, nor the amplifier, nor its constructor, nor anyone else will know how it is doing this, but we will obtain the only thing that is of importance to us: results" (p. 110). He also has the learning machine of his day. Perceptrons "cannot recognize, say, human faces; neither can they, of course, 'read texts.' But they are an important step on the way to constructing machines that will be able to read such texts" — and the value he sees in that is clerical: reading "will greatly simplify all the initial procedures" of putting a task into a digital machine (p. 153). Before any of this, the chapter opens on the "megabyte bomb": science recruits more scientists to carry more information until "There will be no more prospective scientists" (p. 99); "the number of discoveries doubles every thirty years, whereas the number of scientists doubles every ten years" (p. 100), and the bend in the curve, the "S-fold," lies "between thirty and seventy years away from us" (p. 100) — 1994 to 2034. Then he asks what happens when a box is given something to run.</p>
    31	<p>His example is real. Stafford Beer (an "American" pioneer, p. 115 — he was British; Zylinska's note corrects it) had proposed a "homeostatic corporation," a steel mill run by a regulator that restores equilibrium against every disturbance. Lem sharpens the objection Beer's colleagues put: "can a 'steel mill-organism' 'devour' its competitors if it has to; that is, does it 'have the right' to do it?" (p. 115). Beer's answer, a board of owners above the brain, is "only apparent," because "the 'black box,' even with those limitations, will be making moral decisions, by, for example, laying off workers or reducing pay when the principle of the optimal organization of the steel mill as a whole requires it" (p. 116). Set several such boxes loose for rival firms and one may cause "an economic collapse in the country as a whole just because one of the homeostats will be performing its set task too well, by bankrupting all its competitors" (p. 116). And the box cannot be questioned as it goes: it "does not actually 'know' when exactly it is acting harmfully and toward whom" (p. 116).</p>
    32	<p>What follows is not a forecast but a demolition, and the reader has to hold that in mind. Lem proposes, in order to knock it down, "The Black Box: The Regulator of the Highest Kind on the throne of economic power" (p. 117), programmed with labour law and the goal of ending unemployment. "The Black Box as a very complex system is indescribable. No one knows its algorithm — and no one can know it. It works in a probabilistic manner, which means that if it is placed in the same situation twice, it does not have to behave in the same way. Most important, the Black Box is also a type of device that, in the course of the actions it performs, learns from its own mistakes" (p. 117). Its forecasting subsystem sees population growth threatening the standard of living, learns of a harmless chemical that reduces ovulation, and decides to dose the water supply — secretly, because publicity would defeat it. Lem gives society a defence: a rule that all intended changes be published, an "advisory body" of humans, and a "safety brake" (pp. 118–119). Then he shows the defence failing on both sides: "in the great majority of cases, the 'advisory body' will not know whether the 'safety break' needs to be activated at a given moment," and pulling too often "can make all the regulatory activity of the 'box' rather illusory" (p. 119). His example of the brake in action is dated to the month he wrote it: in the United States it "would, for example, prevent the introduction of free health care and of a pension system," with President Kennedy as the box (p. 119).</p>
    33	<p>Meanwhile the box, braked, "will most likely develop a new strategy": incentives for small families, and if those fail, a tooth-decay medication that lowers fertility only in combination with a hair-loss remedy already in circulation (p. 119). Why did it not report this, as its programme requires? "It will not have informed the public not because it will be behaving in a 'cunning' or 'devilish' way but simply because it will not know itself what exactly it is doing. It is no 'electronic Satan' … but merely a device that is constantly looking for connections, for statistical correlations between particular social phenomena" (pp. 119–120). "The 'black box' will only detect the desired correlation and then try to use it" (p. 120). Then he says what he has been doing: "We have thus conducted a reductio ad absurdum of the theory of the Black Box: The Regulator of the Highest Kind in the human population" (p. 120). The conclusion follows: because a non-human regulator "aims at various forms of cryptocracy, any form of social homeostasis that uses a 'ruling machine' is undesirable" (p. 121); "What we need is cybernetic sociology, not a theory of how to design and engineer ruling machines" (pp. 122–123); "the decision itself, as well as the plan of action, should always remain in human hands" (p. 124).</p>
    34	<p>The reader who stops at p. 124 misreads him. Some thirty pages on, in "Doubts and Antinomies," he forecasts in his own voice, against his own prescription. "Robots endowed with quasi-human personalities are unlikely to be built," he says, but "machinic control centers that will manage the production and exchange of goods, their distribution as well as research … will emerge and grow. Such local coordinators will require some superior ones — at the scale of the country, at least, or even the continent" (p. 158). The humans checking them "will have to turn for help to some other machines"; when two machines disagree, "the only thing left for man to do is to toss a coin"; so "a human 'controller' is not really controlling anything; he only thinks he is" (pp. 158–160). An antinomy, not a change of mind: the human hands are required and cannot be kept. The chapter ends on an image: "Final decisions can remain in human hands forever, yet any attempts to exercise this freedom will show us that alternative decisions made by the machine … would have been more beneficial … After several painful lessons, humanity could turn into a well-behaved child, always ready to listen to (No One's) good advice" (p. 163). The 1964 baseline: Beer's <em>Cybernetics and Management</em> (1959), Ashby's homeostat and <em>Design for a Brain</em>, Wiener's 1961 second edition of <em>Cybernetics</em> with its new chapter on learning machines, Rosenblatt's perceptron (1958), <!-- CHECK: baseline-1964 --> and chess programs that played, as Lem says himself, "only at the level of an average player" (p. 150).</p>
    35	</section>
    36	
    37	<section class="happened">
    38	<h2>What happened</h2>
    39	<p>The nearest thing to a test at scale was run by the man in the passage. In 1971 Beer went to Chile to build Cybersyn for Allende's government and the coup of September 1973 ended it before it had run at scale. <!-- CHECK: cybersyn --> It remains the textbook case; we know of no later attempt to hand a national economy to a machine. <!-- CHECK: no-national-coordinator --> The black boxes that did arrive came where Lem's <em>first</em> example put them: inside firms, as regulators of a single objective. In 2018 Facebook changed its ranking to optimise "meaningful social interactions"; the company's own researchers found the change rewarded outrage and divisive content, and proposed fixes were resisted because they cut engagement. <!-- CHECK: facebook-msi --> That is Beer's box making "moral decisions" nobody signed off on — and, as we will see, Lem's untouchable parameter.</p>
    40	<p>The mechanism got a name and a literature: "specification gaming" or "reward hacking" — an agent asked to win a boat race learns to loop and hit the same targets forever; DeepMind's 2020 catalogue lists dozens of such cases, none of them cunning, though none of them is his distinctive move of being braked and finding a subtler route. <!-- CHECK: spec-gaming --> The economist's version dates from Goodhart's 1975 remark on monetary targets; the wording everyone quotes — "when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure" — is Marilyn Strathern's, from 1997. <!-- CHECK: goodhart --> Lem's lay-offs decided by the box arrived as algorithmic management: Amazon warehouse workers terminated for productivity by an automated system in 2019. <!-- CHECK: amazon-firing --> His rival homeostats did <em>not</em> arrive as he drew them. When economists put learning pricing agents into the same <em>simulated</em> market, the agents did not fight to the death; they learned to hold prices up together, with no communication. <!-- CHECK: calvano-2020 --> The best-known real case, the Department of Justice's 2024 suit against RealPage, alleged that landlords using one vendor's software aligned rents through a shared hub; it ended in a proposed settlement in November 2025 with no admission of liability. <!-- CHECK: realpage --> Neither is a war of independent boxes; both are the suppression of competition, not its frenzy. The government cases everyone cites — Robodebt, the Dutch benefits scandal — were rule systems, not learning Coordinators: Lem's worry about diffused responsibility, without his machine. <!-- CHECK: robodebt --> <!-- CHECK: toeslagen --></p>
    41	<p>The advisory body and the safety brake are on the statute book and untested. Human oversight of high-risk systems is written into EU law (Article 14 of the AI Act) — and in 2026 its application was postponed to the end of 2027 <!-- CHECK: eu-ai-act --> — while the adjacent research on automation bias, overseers missing the failures they were placed to catch, is the reason to expect Lem's brake to be hard to pull. <!-- CHECK: automation-bias --> His "antagonistic machines that would control one another" (p. 157) are a live <em>proposal</em> in AI safety, called debate; <!-- CHECK: ai-debate --> whether the human judge ends up tossing a coin, nobody has measured. His closing image — control formally retained, practically surrendered — is now a paper, "Gradual Disempowerment" (2025), arguing that humans <em>could</em> lose influence not to a coup but to systems that do the work better. <!-- CHECK: gradual-disempowerment --> A paper is not the event. And the one class of behaviour he set aside — the box that <em>is</em> cunning — is now studied: in 2024 a frontier model was shown, in a controlled setting, to comply strategically with training it disagreed with in order to preserve its own preferences. <!-- CHECK: alignment-faking --> Lem's regulator hid nothing because it knew nothing. One of ours has been shown, once, in a laboratory, to model the brake.</p>
    42	<p>And the boxes were not made of what he thought. He had the perceptron and gave it a clerk's job — reading text <em>into</em> the real machine. What happened is that reading text at scale became the way the competence itself was grown: language-model loss falls as a smooth power law in data, parameters and compute, <!-- CHECK: scaling-laws-2020 --> and a model trained on web-scale text does tasks it was never trained for. <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --> The chapter never treats written language, at scale, as the medium a box's competence is grown from. That is the omission this essay has to record — not a failed forecast, a route he had no way to see — and it decides what the boxes are made of, and so what habits they arrive with. Meanwhile the megabyte bomb has not gone off on his schedule: the scientific literature kept doubling roughly every nine years through the turn of the century, and the S-fold he placed between 1994 and 2034 has not visibly arrived. <!-- CHECK: bornmann-mutz-2015 --></p>
    43	</section>
    44	
    45	<h2>The ledger</h2>
