    61	<tr><td>Machines controlling machines; when they disagree, "toss a coin" (pp. 157–160)</td><td>Debate is a proposal; no measurement of what human judges do when overseer-models disagree <!-- CHECK: ai-debate --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    62	<tr><td>Harm comes "not because it will be behaving in a 'cunning' or 'devilish' way" (p. 119); no revolt, no "Electronic Antichrist" (p. 107)</td><td>The harm cases in this essay are his kind; strategic compliance shown once, in a controlled study, 2024 — not a revolt <!-- CHECK: alignment-faking --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    63	<tr><td>Ending: control retained in name, surrendered in practice; humanity "a well-behaved child, always ready to listen to (No One's) good advice" (p. 163)</td><td>"Gradual Disempowerment" (2025) states the scenario; nothing yet shows it has happened <!-- CHECK: gradual-disempowerment --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    64	<tr><td>Prescription: "cybernetic sociology, not … ruling machines" (pp. 122–123)</td><td>No state has delegated rule to a learning system; rule-based automation (Robodebt, toeslagenaffaire) shows what diffused responsibility costs without Lem's machine <!-- CHECK: robodebt --> <!-- CHECK: toeslagen --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
