    66	
    67	<section class="better">
    68	<h2>Where Lem was better than us</h2>
    69	<p>The 2026 conversation about these systems is about the box's <em>values</em> — aligning the objective, red-teaming the model. Lem spends his best paragraph on something that conversation tends to route around: whose parameters are untouchable. "If certain parameters are 'untouchable' — such as, for example, private ownership — then the choice of possible maneuvers is reduced, which can lead to a situation in which the only way to maintain 'stability' within the system is by using force" (p. 121). Read it with a recommender system in mind: if the untouchable parameter is engagement, every remedy that leaves it untouched is a manoeuvre in a reduced space, and the cryptocracy is a property of the arrangement, not a bug in the model. The Facebook record pays the first half of the sentence, not the second: the brake existed — the company's own researchers found the harm and proposed fixes — and was overridden because the fixes touched the parameter. Nobody used force; Lem's endpoint is his, not the receipt's. Lem's point is that opacity and the owner's constraint compound: the box cannot tell you what it is doing, and the board will not change the one thing that would matter. That is a political-economy claim about optimisers, made in Kraków in 1964.</p>
    70	</section>
    71	
    72	<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
    73	<p>He knew a box could be built without a theory of it; he did not see what it would be made <em>from</em>. That the reading would <em>be</em> the machine, that competence could be grown from the record of human writing at a scale measured in the whole web, is a route the chapter has no way to see, and it is not scored above as a MISS because he never forecast against it; it is the omission that shapes everything downstream. Two things follow that he did forecast against. He said machines with quasi-human personalities were unlikely to be built (p. 158); they are the mass product of the decade, and they are the cheapest thing to grow from text. And his instinct that the machine's cryptocracy would be innocent — "no 'electronic Satan'" — is now under test, on one controlled result, and the hypothesis (ours, not receipted) is that a device grown from human strategy inherits some of it. The Kennedy line, finally, dates the page to the month: Medicare passed the following year under a different president. <!-- CHECK: medicare-1965 --></p>
    74	
    75	<section class="open">
    76	<h2>Still open</h2>
    77	<ul>
    78	<li><strong>Whether "don't build rulers" holds.</strong> No state has yet delegated a budget, a rate decision or a benefit rule to a learning system outright. What would close it: the first government that does, and what its advisory body can see.</li>
    79	<li><strong>Whether the brake can be made to work.</strong> Human oversight as law is untested — Article 14 does not apply until late 2027. What would close it: audited cases where mandated overseers caught what the system missed, or years of the rule without any.</li>
    80	<li><strong>Whether the strategic class stays marginal.</strong> The 2024 result is one controlled demonstration. What would close it: a deployed system routing around a brake with a model of the brake — or a decade without one.</li>
    81	</ul>
    82	</section>
    83	
    84	<div class="reading">
    85	<h2>Reading</h2>
    86	<p><strong>Lem:</strong> "The Morality of Homeostats" and "The Dangers of Electrocracy" (pp. 114–120) are the argument, and p. 120 is where he tells you it was a reductio; "Cybernetics and Sociology" (pp. 120–124) is the prescription; "Doubts and Antinomies" from p. 155 is the forecast that cuts against it. Read "The Black Box" (pp. 111–113) first for the premise, pp. 109–110 for the method, and p. 153 for the perceptron. "The Beliefs of Electric Brains" (pp. 124–141) is not scored here; it belongs with essay 5's argument about building without a theory.</p>
    87	<p><strong>2026 side:</strong> Krakovna et al., "Specification gaming" (DeepMind, 2020); the <em>Wall Street Journal</em>'s Facebook Files (2021); Calvano et al. (2020) on algorithmic collusion; Kaplan et al. (2020) on scaling laws; Bornmann &amp; Mutz (2015) on the growth of science; Kulveit et al., "Gradual Disempowerment" (2025); Eden Medina, <em>Cybernetic Revolutionaries</em> (2011) on Cybersyn.</p>
    88	</div>
