   135	### 7. The megabyte-bomb row receipts the wrong series and is generous
   136	
   137	**Location:** “There will be no more prospective scientists”; S-fold “between thirty and seventy years away” (pp. 99–100) → “Publication growth still roughly exponential (doubling ~9 years) into the 2010s; his window runs to 2034” → `OPEN`. Prose: “the S-fold he placed between 1994 and 2034 has not visibly arrived.”
   138	
   139	**Problem:** p. 99 is a scientist-headcount barrier. p. 100’s S-fold is the bend in *scientist* exponential growth, 1994–2034. Bornmann & Mutz 2015 (and the 2021 update in the register) measure *publications*. That cannot pay “no more prospective scientists” or “the S-fold has not arrived.” `OPEN` because the calendar still says 2034 is defensible as a dating hedge; it is not defensible as a claim that the scientist-limit is still live. We did not run out of scientists.
   140	
   141	**Fix:** Receipt researcher headcount, or drop the scientist clause. If the row stays on the S-fold of publications, say so and do not call that his claim. A fairer pair is `RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER` (information overload is real; the binding constraint was not “no more prospective scientists”) or `MISS` on the headcount barrier, with the 2034 window stated as not yet closed for the *shape* of the curve only.
   142	
   143	---
   144	
   145	### 8. Automation bias is not the p. 119 failure, and is scored `HIT`
   146	
   147	**Location:** advisory body “will not know whether the ‘safety break’ needs to be activated” (p. 119) → “Automation-bias studies: overseers miss the automation failures they were placed to catch” → `HIT`. Prose: “the reason to expect Lem’s brake to be hard to pull.”
   148	
   149	**Problem:** Lem’s failure is epistemic: the box’s intended change is opaque, so the board cannot tell *whether* to fire. Automation bias is deference to a recommendation the human could in principle evaluate. Related family; not “substantially as described.” `HIT` here is a cousin credited as the thing.
   150	
   151	**Fix:** `EARLY` or `RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER`, and say the 2026 literature is deference, not inability to see the plan. Or replace the receipt with a documented case where overseers could not tell *whether* a learning system’s proposed action was the one that needed the brake.
   152	
   153	---
   154	
   155	### 9. Facebook pays an “untouchable parameter” `HIT` that Lem attached to private ownership and a state regulator
   156	
   157	**Location:** “If certain parameters are ‘untouchable’ — such as, for example, private ownership — then the choice of possible maneuvers is reduced” (p. 121) → Facebook 2018, fixes resisted because they cut engagement → `HIT`. “Where Lem was better than us” then rereads the sentence “with a recommender system in mind.”
   158	
   159	**Problem:** The force half was correctly dropped. What remains is still not the same object. Lem’s parameter is a political-economic constraint on a *social* regulator (his example: the mill’s owners blocking nationalization). Facebook’s is a firm KPI. Reduced manoeuvre space is the analogy; `HIT` needs the thing substantially as described. The “better than us” section is where the conversion happens: the reader is told to substitute engagement for private ownership.
   160	
   161	**Fix:** Keep Facebook as an illustration in prose. In the ledger, either `RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER` (the live dilemma is which parameters a board will not touch; his instance was ownership/force) or find a receipted case of a public regulator whose legally untouchable constraints collapsed the option set.
   162	
   163	---
   164	
   165	### 10. Alignment-faking is made to “model the brake”
   166	
   167	**Location:** “One of ours has been shown, once, in a laboratory, to model the brake.” Ledger row is already `OPEN` and more careful.
   168	
   169	**Problem:** Greenblatt et al. 2024 is strategic compliance with a training objective in a constructed setting, with a hidden scratchpad. It is not a regulator modeling an advisory-body safety brake and routing around it. The still-open bullet later asks for exactly that field event. The prose spends the result early.
   170	
   171	**Fix:** Keep the controlled demonstration, as the ledger does. Cut “model the brake,” or mark the gloss as the essay’s, not the paper’s.
   172	
   173	---
   174	
   175	### 11. Layers: Facebook is Beer’s box in prose, a p. 121 parameter in the table
   176	
   177	**Location:** “That is Beer’s box making ‘moral decisions’ nobody signed off on — and, as we will see, Lem’s untouchable parameter.”
   178	
   179	**Problem:** Leadership signed off on the MSI ranking change. The outrage consequence is what they did not want. The ledger now uses `facebook-msi` only at problem level (untouchable engagement). The sentence puts the same receipt in Beer’s object-level mill *and* the p. 121 problem. Object and problem in one breath.
   180	
   181	**Fix:** One job for the receipt. If it pays p. 121, stop calling the feed Beer’s homeostat.
   182	
   183	---
   184	
   185	### 12. Amazon `RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON` is still a generic surface match
   186	
   187	**Location:** Beer’s homeostat “laying off workers… when the principle of the optimal organization of the steel mill as a whole requires it” (p. 116) → “Automated productivity terminations at Amazon, 2019 — a metric threshold, not a firm-wide regulator.”
   188	
   189	**Problem:** The 2026 cell admits the object is not his. The “right answer” that remains is “a machine fired people.” That is the vague idea credited with the specific box. The verdict is the one the last panel asked for; it is still more generous than “substantially as described.”
   190	
   191	**Fix:** Narrow the Lem cell to metric-triggered dismissal, or move the row to problem level (`RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER`: automated labour allocation is live; the whole-mill moral homeostat is not).
   192	
   193	---
   194	
   195	### 13. 2026 claims to challenge (in addition to 2, 4, 7, 10)
   196	
   197	| Claim | Why |
   198	|---|---|
   199	| “the S-fold… has not visibly arrived” | Paid by a publication series; his S-fold is scientist growth. |
   200	| “No state has yet delegated a budget, a rate decision or a benefit rule to a learning system outright” | Unmarked in still-open; stronger than `no-national-coordinator`; toeslagen is a counter-example unless “outright” is defined tightly enough to exclude it, and then the definition must be in the sentence. |
   201	| “they are the cheapest thing to grow from text” | Unmarked; not what Kaplan or GPT-3 show. |
   202	| “the black boxes that did arrive came where Lem’s *first* example put them: inside firms, as regulators of a single objective” | Interpretive, unmarked; recommenders and warehouse thresholds are not Beer’s ultrastable mill. |
   203	| Article 14 as Lem’s advisory body / brake “on the statute book” | High-risk human oversight is a legal cousin of one component (a human in the loop), not publication-of-all-changes + advisory body + brake. The postponement is correctly stated. |
   204	
   205	---
   206	
   207	### 14. Reader
   208	
   209	A skeptic of “Lem predicted everything” will take the reductio framing, the narrowed spec-gaming row, and the `OPEN`s on debate, cunning, disempowerment, and Coordinators-at-country-scale. They will not take the personality `MISS` once they read past the em-dash on p. 158, will not accept ChatGPT’s 800M as persona-robots, will notice the local-coordinator clause was left off the table, and will treat Facebook→untouchable-parameter and automation-bias→advisory-body as the conversion step. The ledger still does some of the persuading; the remaining `HIT`s and the one `MISS` are where it stops.
     1	**1. Misreading Lem & Verdict Inflation (Severity: High)**
     2	* **Location:** Ledger row: `"Robots endowed with quasi-human personalities are unlikely to be built" (p. 158)`
     3	* **Problem:** The quote is truncated to manufacture an unfair MISS. Lem's very next words are "—unless it is for the kinds of purposes imagined by Fritz Leiber in his novel *The Silver Egghead* (1961)... brothels with electronic ladies." The 2026 receipt cites "companion apps as a product category" to fail him, but Lem explicitly carved out this exact exception for sex/companion robots. 
     4	* **Fix:** Restore the full quote including the Fritz Leiber exception. Change the verdict to HIT or EARLY, as he correctly identified the specific drive that would get these personalities built.
     5	
     6	**2. The Two Layers & Verdict Inflation (Severity: High)**
     7	* **Location:** Ledger row: `Beer's homeostat "laying off workers or reducing pay when the principle of the optimal organization … requires it"`
     8	* **Problem:** This row mixes the object-level prediction (a firm-wide regulator) with the problem-level outcome (automated firing). Amazon's system is a narrow stopwatch metric, not a learning "homeostat" making moral decisions to balance an organization's economy. Placing this in the *Object level* and scoring it RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON for the outcome launders the failure of the object itself.
     9	* **Fix:** Move this to the Problem level if judging the dilemma of automated firing, or keep it in the Object level and score it as a MISS since the firm-wide learning regulator did not arrive.
    10	
    11	**3. Missing MISS (Severity: Medium)**
    12	* **Location:** Prose section "Where he was worst": "He knew a box could be built without a theory of it; he did not see what it would be made from... it is not scored above as a MISS because he never forecast against it"
    13	* **Problem:** The essay claims Lem didn't forecast the substrate, excusing him from a MISS. However, on p. 109, Lem *did* make a specific object-level forecast for the substrate: a colony of ciliates, colloids, or multiphase solutions. That the black box was built from math and silicon is a direct MISS for his ciliate/colloid prediction, which the essay skips.
    14	* **Fix:** Add an Object-level row for the ciliate/colloid/multiphase solution substrate (p. 109) and score it as a MISS.
    15	
    16	**4. Verdict Inflation (Severity: Medium)**
    17	* **Location:** Ledger row: `"If certain parameters are 'untouchable' — such as, for example, private ownership..."`
    18	* **Problem:** The essay scores a HIT by equating Lem's macroeconomic constraint ("private ownership") with a modern product metric ("Facebook engagement"). This credits an analogy as a specific prediction, inflating the verdict for a vague idea.
    19	* **Fix:** Change the verdict to RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER. The dilemma of untouchable parameters exists, but the actual parameters causing harm turned out to be product metrics, not macro-political ones.
    20	
    21	**5. Verdict Inflation (Severity: Low)**
    22	* **Location:** Ledger row: `Perceptrons "cannot recognize, say, human faces; neither can they … 'read texts.' But they are an important step" toward machines that will`
    23	* **Problem:** The receipt justifies the HIT by stating "reading text at scale became the training route itself (GPT-3)". Lem predicted machines reading text as a *clerical task* (OCR) to save operators the time of translating tasks into machine language (p. 153). Using GPT-3's scale training to validate his prediction of OCR conflates his narrow prediction with a completely different mechanism.
    24	* **Fix:** Keep the HIT for deep networks reading text (OCR), but remove the reference to GPT-3 and scale training from the receipt.
    25	
    26	**6. Reader Persuasion (Severity: Low)**
    27	* **Location:** The ledger's overall rhetorical approach.
    28	* **Problem:** A skeptic of the "Lem predicted everything" claim would not be persuaded by this ledger. The ledger relies heavily on analogy (private ownership = engagement) and truncation (ignoring the companion robot exception) to secure its verdicts. The prose does the heavy lifting ("That is a political-economy claim about optimisers...") to make metaphorical matches look like direct predictions.
    29	* **Fix:** Clean up the ledger strictly per the `AGENT.md` rules: no analogies, no truncated quotes, and strict separation of objects from problems. An uninflated ledger is much more persuasive.
    30	
    31	**7. 2026 Claims Check (Severity: None)**
    32	* **Location:** Factual claims about the present throughout the text.
    33	* **Problem:** None. Every factual claim about the present (e.g., Robodebt, EU AI Act timeline, alignment faking study, Calvano 2020) is correctly marked with a `<!-- CHECK -->` marker, and none are wrong or overstated to my knowledge.
    34	* **Fix:** No action needed.
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Search the Summa corpus and print page + section for every hit.

    scripts/find.py "intelligence amplifier"
    scripts/find.py -p 107            # dump printed page 107
    scripts/find.py -s "The Black Box" # dump one section

Use this to write checks/quotes.tsv rows: the page it prints is the page the
row must cite. Matching is whitespace- and quote-mark-insensitive, like verify.sh.
"""
import re, sys
from corpus import load, norm

pages, sections = load()

def page_section(n):
    cur = "?"
    for start, title in sections:
        if start <= n: cur = title
        else: break
    return cur

if len(sys.argv) < 2:
    print(__doc__); sys.exit(0)
if sys.argv[1] == "-p":
    n = int(sys.argv[2]); print("== p.%d [%s]" % (n, page_section(n))); print(pages[n]); sys.exit(0)
if sys.argv[1] == "-s":
    want = sys.argv[2].lower()
    for i, (start, title) in enumerate(sections):
        if title.lower() == want:
            end = sections[i + 1][0] if i + 1 < len(sections) else len(pages)
            for n in range(start, end):
                print("== p.%d" % n); print(pages[n])
            sys.exit(0)
    print("no section named", sys.argv[2]); sys.exit(1)

needle = norm(" ".join(sys.argv[1:]))
hits = 0
for n, text in pages.items():
    flat = norm(text)
    i = flat.find(needle)
    while i >= 0:
        hits += 1
        ctx = flat[max(0, i - 80): i + len(needle) + 80]
        print("p.%-4d [%s]\n    …%s…" % (n, page_section(n), ctx))
        i = flat.find(needle, i + 1)
print("%d hit(s)" % hits)

exec
/bin/bash -lc "rg -n '"'^(chatgpt-scale|companion-apps|toeslagen|bornmann-mutz-2015|automation-bias|facebook-msi|amazon-firing|eu-ai-act|alignment-faking|gpt3-2020|cybersyn|no-national-coordinator)'"\\t' checks/claims-2026.tsv && scripts/find.py -p 99 && scripts/find.py -p 100 && scripts/find.py -p 107 && scripts/find.py -p 109 && scripts/find.py -p 110 && scripts/find.py -p 115 && scripts/find.py -p 116 && scripts/find.py -p 117 && scripts/find.py -p 118 && scripts/find.py -p 119 && scripts/find.py -p 120 && scripts/find.py -p 121 && scripts/find.py -p 153 && scripts/find.py -p 157 && scripts/find.py -p 158 && scripts/find.py -p 159 && scripts/find.py -p 160 && scripts/find.py -p 163" in /home/diablo/book15
 exited 1 in 118ms:
exec
/bin/bash -lc "sed -n '1,80p' checks/claims-2026.tsv && rg -n 'chatgpt-scale|companion-apps|toeslagen|bornmann|automation-bias|facebook-msi|amazon-firing|eu-ai-act|alignment-faking|gpt3-2020|cybersyn|no-national' checks/claims-2026.tsv" in /home/diablo/book15
