     1	# Book 15 — CONTEXT (authority document)
     2	
     3	**Working title: *Sixty Years Later*.** Ten essays reading Stanisław Lem's *Summa
     4	Technologiae* (1964) from 2026: chapter by chapter, where he was right, wrong, right for
     5	the wrong reason, or asking a question that has since dissolved or is still open.
     6	Lem graded himself in "Twenty Years Later" (1982) and again in the 1990s; this book is
     7	the next grading, by a reader who has the things Lem only argued about.
     8	
     9	Precedence (inherited from book8/book12): this file → AGENT.md (reader, priority stack,
    10	verdict rules, pitch gate) → essay contract (§6) → TEMPLATE.md → prose.
    11	
    12	## 1. Thesis
    13	
    14	Lem asked to be judged on *problems*, not gadgets: he wrote that futurology which
    15	predicts devices is astrology, and that the honest job is to map which questions a
    16	technological civilization will be forced to face. So the scoring here is two-layered.
    17	**Layer A — the object level:** did the thing he described come to exist (VR, gene
    18	editing, machine intelligence, information farming, the SETI silence)? **Layer B — the
    19	problem level:** did the *dilemma* he attached to it become the live dilemma, or a
    20	different one, or none? A chapter can score high on B with a zero on A (phantomatics:
    21	headsets are a niche, but the "can you tell?" argument is now a philosophy industry) and
    22	the reverse (his cosmology detail vs the actual Fermi debate). The verdict vocabulary
    23	(§4) exists so the essays never collapse into "prophet / not prophet."
    24	
    25	The book's own claim: Lem's *misses* are the more instructive half. Where he was wrong
    26	he was wrong in a specific, dated way (1960s cybernetics; information theory as the master
    27	science; colloids and ciliates rather than silicon as the substrate of the black box; the
    28	"human-producing" machine judged "incomparably more difficult" than the "travel" one,
    29	p. 206 — the order 2026 actually got) — and 2026's own blind spots are probably of the same
    30	kind. Note for essay 4: he *did* see learning-from-correlation as the black box's mechanism
    31	(pp. 117–120, 153–54); the miss is language as substrate and scale as method, not statistics.
    32	
    33	## 2. Corpus
    34	
    35	- **Primary:** Lem, *Summa Technologiae*, trans. Joanna Zylinska, Minnesota 2013
    36	  (Electronic Mediations 40), 383 pp. Local: `resources/lem-summa-2013.pdf` →
    37	  `resources/text/summa.txt` (both gitignored; rebuild with `scripts/build-corpus.sh`).
    38	  PDF page N == printed page N; the extraction is quotable and section-mapped.
    39	- **Corpus gap (recorded 2026-08-16):** the 2013 translation carries chapters 1–8 only.
    40	  Chapter 9, *Sztuka i technologia*, is absent, as is the 1982 afterword "Dwadzieścia
    41	  lat później" (quoted only in Zylinska's introduction). Essay 9 needs the Polish
    42	  (Wydawnictwo Literackie, 4th exp. ed. 1984 or later) or the Russian (*Сумма
    43	  технологии*, Мир 1968 / АСТ 2002). Until one is on disk, essay 9 quotes cannot be
    44	  gated and the essay does not ship. **Open: user to supply a copy.**
    45	- **Secondary, Lem:** "Twenty Years Later" (written 1982, appended to the 4th ed.), "Thirty Years Later" (1991),
    46	  *Dialogi* (1957), *Filozofia przypadku* (1968) — for how Lem himself re-scored.
    47	- **2026 side:** whatever the essay's claim needs — receipted per claim in
    48	  `checks/claims-2026.tsv`. No claim about "what happened" rides on memory.
    49	
    50	## 3. Spine — ten essays
    51	
    52	| # | Lem chapter (2013 pp.) | Essay working title | Object-level test |
    53	|---|---|---|---|
    54	| 0 | — (Translator's intro; "Twenty Years Later") | How to grade a prophet | the method itself |
    55	| 1 | 1 Dilemmas (24–30) | Is futurology a science yet? | forecasting/x-risk/AI-timeline culture |
    56	| 2 | 2 Two Evolutions (31–58) | The second evolution, observed | Moore's law, tech-as-organism talk, AI as evolutionary process |
    57	| 3 | 3 Civilizations in the Universe (59–92) | Sixty years of silence | 5,000+ exoplanets, no signal; Great Filter, Dark Forest |
    58	| 4 | 4 Intelectronics (93–163) | The black box arrived | LLMs; the "megabyte bomb"; intelligence amplifier; electrocracy |
    59	| 5 | 5 Prolegomena to Omnipotence (164–196) | Design without understanding | ML as imitology; AlphaFold; "methodological madness" |
    60	| 6 | 6 Phantomology (197–236) | Can you tell? | VR's plateau; simulation argument; deepfakes; BCI |
    61	| 7 | 7 The Creation of Worlds (237–293) | Information farming, at last | AI-for-science, automated discovery; linguistic engineering |
    62	| 8 | 8 A Lampoon of Evolution (294–~350) | Redesigning the designer | CRISPR, He Jiankui, longevity, prosthetics; the ESP misstep |
    63	| 9 | 9 Art and Technology (PL/RU only) | The generated image | generative art, music, text — corpus gap, see §2 |
    64	
    65	Order = Lem's order. Essay 0 sets the scoring rules and is written *last* (it must
