     1	# AGENT.md — instructions for the writing model (book 15)
     2	
     3	## Who is reading
     4	An adult who has heard "Lem predicted everything" and is suspicious of it. They have used
     5	an LLM, may own a headset, read the news about CRISPR babies. They have not read *Summa*
     6	and will not — the essay must carry enough Lem, verbatim, that they don't need to. They
     7	will check a claim if it looks too neat.
     8	
     9	## Priority stack (higher wins on conflict)
    10	1. **Never false.** Lem quoted verbatim, paged, gated. 2026 claims receipted. No numbers
    11	   from memory. Being boring is a failure; being wrong is a betrayal.
    12	2. **Grade, don't celebrate.** Every essay must contain at least one MISS or RIGHT
    13	   QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER, found honestly. If none exists, the essay says why that
    14	   chapter is different — and the panel is told to attack that.
    15	3. **The two layers stay separate.** Object-level and problem-level are scored in
    16	   different ledger rows. "He got the idea" never launders "he got the thing wrong."
    17	4. **The reader must be able to feel 1964.** Give the state of the art Lem was
    18	   extrapolating from (vacuum tubes to transistors, Ashby, Shannon, Wiener, Sputnik-era
    19	   SETI) so the reader can see the distance, not just the endpoint.
    20	
    21	## Verdict rules
    22	- Use CONTEXT §4 vocabulary exactly. One verdict per row. Rows are specific + paged.
    23	- When Lem re-graded himself (1982/1991), score against the later Lem too and say so.
    24	- Distinguish *Lem said* from *Zylinska translated*; check the Polish term when the
    25	  English is doing work (imitology, phantomatics, "intelectronics").
    26	- The "still open" section is not a dumping ground: each open item names what
    27	  evidence would close it.
    28	- Score Lem's *arguments* as arguments. When he builds a case to knock it down (he
    29	  says "reductio" on p. 120; the Superphantomat p. 207–8 is another), the object-level
    30	  row is not "he predicted X"; the row is the mechanism inside the reductio, at problem
    31	  level. Grade his own-voice forecasts (e.g. Coordinators, p. 158) separately.
    32	- A thing being *reported* is not the thing being *true*: a paper that argues a
    33	  scenario, a lawsuit that alleges a practice, a demo that shows a capability — each
    34	  receipts exactly what it is (a proposal, an allegation, a demo) and no more. HIT needs
    35	  the event; otherwise OPEN or EARLY with the receipt described honestly.
    36	- Rankings are not chronologies; a "harder" is not a "later". Read the comparison
    37	  Lem actually made before scoring what happened to it (panel lesson, essay 6).
    38	
    39	## Pitch before writing (the guinea-pig gate)
    40	Never drafted cold. For each essay present the user 2–4 candidate angles, ≤4 sentences
    41	each: the Lem passage that anchors it (paged), the 2026 fact it meets, and the verdict
    42	tension. The user picks; the rest are banked in `drafts/NN.pitches.md`.
    43	
    44	## Pre-ship test (all five or don't ship)
    45	1. Does the essay quote Lem enough that a reader could disagree with *our reading* of
    46	   him from the quotes alone?
    47	2. Is there a real MISS or WRONG-ANSWER row, argued, not token?
    48	3. Is every 2026 claim marked `<!-- CHECK: id -->` with a non-`open` row?
    49	4. Would someone who admires Lem and someone who thinks he's overrated both find the
    50	   ledger fair? (Panel question.)
    51	5. Dinner test: can the reader say in one sentence what Lem got right *and* wrong here,
    52	   without saying "he was ahead of his time"?
    53	
